Title: "Semantics, Truth Tables, and Tautologies"
Id: "3cc2220f-22ed-4dff-adb7-0ce12e5ac761"


Text: "Syntax defines the combinatorial universe of formulas; semantics assigns
       truth values to those symbolic forms. Classical propositional logic is
       truth-functional: the truth value of a compound proposition is uniquely
       determined by the truth values of its constituent variables."
Id: "086b636f-e282-44fb-bedb-9ee8c7a540f4"


[\truth.value]
Declares: v
satisfies:
. anyOf:
  . v = 0
  . v = 1
Documented:
. called: "Boolean truth value"
. written: "\{0, 1\}"
. description: "A classical Boolean truth value, where 1 denotes truth and 0
                denotes falsity."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{21}
Id: "a73bb4ad-1ea6-4545-828a-95b7d62e54d6"


Text: "A truth assignment assigns a truth value to each atomic variable. By the
       Unique Readability Theorem, this assignment extends uniquely to evaluate
       every well-formed formula."
Id: "a2356352-fa54-4da0-8486-f4010f48cf51"


[\truth.valuation]
Declares: v is (_ is \\statement) -> (_ is \truth.value)
Documented:
. called: "truth valuation"
. written: "v: \text{Var} \to \{0, 1\}"
. description: "A truth assignment on propositional variables extended
                homomorphically to evaluate every formula according to classical
                truth-table equations."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{21}
Id: "fe694d65-86c9-454b-8a04-49d67739cee3"


Text: "A valuation satisfies a formula when the formula evaluates to truth under
       that assignment. Formulas that are true under every valuation are
       tautologies, representing universal laws of logic."
Id: "9b3ea74c-409d-4620-bfab-eb8b05df4ab1"


[v \.models./ A]
States:
when:
. v is \truth.valuation
. A is \\statement
that:
. have: v(A) = 1
Documented:
. called: "valuation $v?$ satisfies formula $A?$"
. written: "v? \models A?"
. description: "The assertion that formula A evaluates to truth (1) under truth
                valuation v."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{22}
Id: "f439fb4e-4a08-46b3-a065-f01f1270f79c"


[\tautology]
Declares: A is \\statement
satisfies:
. forAll: v is \truth.valuation
  then: (v \.models./ A)
Documented:
. called: "tautology"
. written: "\models A?"
. description: "A propositional formula that evaluates to true under every
                conceivable truth valuation."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{22}
Id: "02f33b27-1aaf-4578-8477-fc913288b216"


[\satisfiable]
Declares: A is \\statement
satisfies:
. exists: v is \truth.valuation
  suchThat: (v \.models./ A)
Documented:
. called: "satisfiable formula"
. description: "A formula that evaluates to true under at least one truth
                valuation."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{22}
Id: "841a33c5-ddd0-4f99-9c29-07653fd9d74e"


Text: "Two formulas are logically equivalent when they yield identical truth
       values across all valuations. Logical equivalence partitions formulas
       into a Boolean algebra under conjunction, disjunction, and negation."
Id: "cea67ba0-bac3-419d-b94e-5b560a3cb798"


[A \.logically.equivalent./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \\statement
that:
. forAll: v is \truth.valuation
  then: (v(A) = v(B))
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ is logically equivalent to $B?$"
. written: "A? \equiv B?"
. description: "Formulas A and B that assume identical truth values under every
                truth valuation."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{24}
Id: "128c1a2f-3b26-40c7-ab77-ae953e1d6b7c"


Text: "Every truth-functional formula can be reduced to canonical normal forms:
       Disjunctive Normal Form (a disjunction of conjunctions of literals) and
       Conjunctive Normal Form (a conjunction of disjunctions of literals)."
Id: "9d73f2e4-d28b-4442-81ea-edad5e59f7b5"


[\in.disjunctive.normal.form]
Declares: A is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "formula in disjunctive normal form"
. written: "A? \in \mathrm{DNF}"
. description: "A formula expressed as a disjunction of clauses, each consisting
                of a conjunction of literals."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{50}
Id: "ff8b2ea5-38ab-4535-a106-f5c48ec6d26a"


[\in.conjunctive.normal.form]
Declares: A is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "formula in conjunctive normal form"
. written: "A? \in \mathrm{CNF}"
. description: "A formula expressed as a conjunction of clauses, each consisting
                of a disjunction of literals."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{50}
Id: "107f67ae-7f59-41eb-92d3-4d203e8766ac"


Theorem:
given: A is \\statement
then:
. exists: D is \in.disjunctive.normal.form
  suchThat:
  . exists: C is \in.conjunctive.normal.form
    suchThat:
    . A \.logically.equivalent./ D
    . A \.logically.equivalent./ C
Documented:
. called: "existence of disjunctive and conjunctive normal forms"
. description: "Every propositional formula is logically equivalent to a formula
                in Disjunctive Normal Form (DNF) and to a formula in Conjunctive
                Normal Form (CNF)."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{50}
Id: "413566b8-93dc-4fc9-914b-e2f1cf635e04"


Text: "A set of connectives is functionally complete when it suffices to
       represent every finite Boolean truth function. The classical connectives
       can be reduced to minimal bases, including singleton Sheffer stroke
       (NAND) and Peirce arrow (NOR)."
Id: "0f072328-775d-49ff-8c67-6582858fa465"


Theorem:
given: A is \\statement
then:
. exists: D is \in.disjunctive.normal.form
  suchThat: A \.logically.equivalent./ D
Documented:
. called: "functional completeness theorem"
. description: "Every Boolean function can be expressed by formulas using
                exclusively {not, and, or}, {not, and}, {not, implies}, the
                Sheffer stroke (NAND), or the Peirce arrow (NOR)."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{52}
Id: "ef197ba1-bdc1-4db9-b93b-d25f29350441"
